    summary:  "Two features, sequenced back-to-back per direct request ('after this is done plan and execute...').\n\nFeature 1 — Multi-Routing-Per-Contract. Researched how SeaRates, CargoSphere, CargoWise, and Freightos model a carrier contract that covers one lane via several distinct physical paths (e.g. HLCU/Kuehne+Nagel CNCKG->SEGOT, bookable via three different transshipment hubs — DEHAM, NLRTM, or Wilhelmshaven — each with its own price and transit time) — this app's contracts previously modeled exactly one leg chain and one rate set per contract, so a second routing on the same lane needed a whole duplicate contract record with no way to compare them side by side. New contract_routings table (a named, ordered bundle: name, transit days, notes) that contract_legs and contract_rates optionally belong to via a new routing_id column (blank = today's exact ungrouped behavior, unaffected). findMatchingContractLeg was reworked into findMatchingContractLegs — groups legs by routing_id, walks the existing chain-matching algorithm per group, and returns every successful run instead of stopping at the first — so GET /api/contracts/match now emits one comparable result per (contract, routing) pair for a multi-routing contract, each with its own scoped rates (that routing's own rows plus contract-wide routing_id='' rows, e.g. a flat documentation fee). A leg/rate correlates to its routing purely by array index into the current save's routings[] payload (routingIndex) — the only identity that survives a save, since routing ids regenerate every time exactly like legs/rates already do — with a NAME-based fallback for a caller that PUTs back {...GET-response, change} without re-deriving indices.\n\nMdmContractsPage.jsx's leg editor is now routing-grouped (new LegCard component), the rate editor gains a conditional Routing column once any routing exists, the old flat 'Routings' modal is renamed 'Route Legs' and mirrors the grouping, and the list gets a routings-count badge. ContractPickerModal (shipment assignment) shows each routing's name/transit days as a distinct pickable option; useContractMismatch and RateBenchmarkPage were both extended to key on routing id, not just contract id, so a mismatch/benchmark check can't silently conflate two differently-priced routings on the same contract. Allocations stay contract-level-only by deliberate design (a space configuration books capacity on a carrier/lane, not a specific physical path) — AllocContractPickerModal dedupes multiple routing-matches back down to one per contract. New tests/contract-routings.test.js (34 assertions).\n\nFeature 2 — Standalone Contract Management Service, with a source toggle. Direct follow-on request: extract the contracts/contract_legs/contract_rates/contract_routings subsystem just built above into its own standalone service CargoDesk calls over HTTP, mirroring the Document Distribution (v0.64.0) and PDF Render (v0.66.0) extractions — but explicitly NOT a cutover like those two: 'leave the current contracts as they are, and add a toggle in the app settings to change the source.' New services/contract-management/ (port 3004, own node:sqlite file, own schema — a faithful line-for-line port of routes/contracts.js's full route surface, including findMatchingContractLegs, publish/withdraw guards, and expireStaleContracts' hourly sweep) runs ALONGSIDE the monolith's own in-process tables rather than replacing them — CargoDesk's first extraction of this shape. A new app_settings.contract_source setting ('local' default | 'remote') decides per-request which one is authoritative; every route in routes/contracts.js and routes/allocations.js's own match endpoint gained one early branch — local falls through to today's exact unchanged code, remote proxies via a new shared callContractService(method, path, body) helper (10s timeout, clean 503 on unreachable, exposed via ctx since — unlike callDistributionService, which only ever had one call site — this toggle needed to reach four different files). server.js's createRateSnapshot/importContractRates and routes/shipment-ops.js's credit-hold check and routes/shipments.js's checkDgPolicy all got the identical branch, since each reads contract_legs/contract_rates/contracts directly rather than through routes/contracts.js's own endpoints — missing any of them would have meant 'remote' mode silently keeping stale local data for cost-line generation, invoice credit holds, or DG-cargo contract checks. The AI Assistant's get_contract tool gained the same branch for consistency. The service owns no linked_ports, shipments, or allocations data of its own: linked-port expansion is resolved by the caller and passed as explicit pairs (confirmed live that table has only a couple of rows, so shipping it whole per request is cheap); the shipment/allocation delete-and-withdraw reference guards are replicated in the monolith's proxy layer, run against its own local tables, before ever calling the remote route.\n\nA real design bug surfaced by testing, not assumed: the service's first draft of findMatchingContractLegs took pre-resolved polLinked/podLinked arrays, but the original algorithm calls linkedPortCodes(leg.pol) — keyed by each LEG's own port, which varies within one contract, not the query's port. Fixed by accepting a linkedPortCodes callback instead. The toggle write itself lives on a new dedicated, admin-only PUT /api/settings/contract-source (not folded into the generic PUT /api/settings, which also allows operator/occ_bk) — same precedent as the existing shipment-sidebar-order route: switching where every contract read and write goes is a different class of action than an ordinary operational setting. Surfaced in AppSettingsPage.jsx's External APIs tab, admin-only, with an explicit one-way-cutover-not-a-live-sync caption. New scripts/migrate-contracts-to-service.js (CLI-only, mirrors seed-contracts.js/checkdb.js's own precedent — reads cargodesk.db directly via node:sqlite, batches into the service's new POST /internal/contracts/bulk-import) is the deliberate, explicit, admin-triggered migration step; flipping the toggle alone never copies data. Dockerfile + docker-compose.yml entry added (undisclosed as build-tested, same standing caveat as the other two services' own Docker artifacts).\n\nNew tests/contract-service-toggle.test.js (20 assertions, the second monolith-level test in this suite requiring two processes running): proves 'local' mode is byte-identical to before this feature, 'remote' mode correctly proxies create/get/publish/match/withdraw AND allocations/match's own per-contract remote-leg resolution, the toggle route is genuinely admin-gated and value-validated, and — the most direct proof this is really two independent datastores, not a sync — a contract created in 'remote' mode is completely invisible once the toggle flips back to 'local', while the original local contracts are untouched throughout. Full 26-file monolith regression suite green, both new service-level test files green (31 assertions), clean production build. Verified live via CDP end-to-end through the real running app: flipped the toggle in Application Settings, confirmed MdmContractsPage.jsx immediately started reading the (empty) remote registry instead of the monolith's own 20 local contracts, created a contract through the real API while in remote mode and confirmed it exists in the standalone service's own database (direct lookup) and nowhere in the monolith's local one, then flipped back and confirmed the original local contracts were exactly as they'd always been.",
