    codename: "Drydock",
    summary:  "Four more architect-review fixes, prompted directly by 'from an architect's perspective, how's our app looking?' following v0.66.0's platform-hardening round. Every finding was independently re-verified against the live codebase before acting — two of the four ARCHITECTURE.md claims driving this round (C1 'no transactions', H5 'no indexes') turned out to be stale/inaccurate as stated, exactly the kind of doc-drift this app has run into repeatedly this session, so the real, narrower gap each one actually had got found and fixed instead of trusting the doc at face value.\n\nIndexes: H5 claimed zero indexes beyond primary keys — 14 already existed. The real gap: shipment_cost_lines, containers, entity_events, and shipment_documents, each queried by shipment_id (or entity_type+entity_id) on every shipment-detail-page load per a direct grep of actual WHERE-clause usage, none indexed. shipment_parties was checked and skipped — its existing UNIQUE(shipment_id, role) constraint already gives an implicit composite index covering the same lookups.\n\nTransactions: C1 claimed no transactions anywhere — 9 BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK blocks already existed. The real gap, found and fixed: routes/contracts.js's saveLegs/saveRates (delete-then-loop-insert contract legs/rates, zero wrapping — an interrupted loop left a contract with its legs/rates fully deleted and only partially replaced); the contract-rate re-import route's overwrite path (same delete-then-regenerate shape); and invoice reversal (loop-insert reversal cost lines then void the original invoice — an interruption risked either a half-reversed invoice or reversal lines created with the original still showing 'confirmed', a real double-counting risk in AR). A handful of lower-stakes candidates (Kanban column reorder, a schedule-removal loop) were found, judged lower priority, and left documented rather than blindly fixed.\n\nMoney-rounding precision: 12 occurrences of the naive Math.round(x*100)/100 cents-rounding pattern across 6 files — a real, verified bug class (Math.round(1.005*100)/100 evaluates to 1, not 1.01, since 1.005*100 is actually 100.49999999999999 in IEEE-754 double arithmetic; confirmed this exact failure and its fix directly before adopting it anywhere). New roundCents() helper (string-exponent decimal shift instead of multiplication) adopted in mapCostLine, toUsd (the core currency-conversion function used throughout the app), and 3 more route files, plus a duplicated copy in the one frontend file affected (no shared module with the backend). Verified byte-identical output for every value the existing test suite already covers, differing only on the specific inputs the naive version got wrong. One real regression caught mid-pass: roundCents was destructured from the mapper factory but never actually added to the ctx object passed to route modules — fixed before it could ship.\n\nProduction deployment: verified zero Dockerfiles and no static-file-serving path existed at all — the monolith could not serve its own built frontend under any circumstances before this. Added NODE_ENV=production-gated static serving + SPA fallback (verified live in a real production-mode run — root, an arbitrary path, and a real built asset all serve correctly; an unmatched /api/* path is correctly NOT swallowed by the SPA catch-all); three Dockerfiles (monolith multi-stage build, plus document-distribution and pdf-render, both built from the repo root context so their hoisted-dependency convention keeps working inside the image); a docker-compose.yml wiring all three together. A real bug caught before shipping: the first draft mounted a named volume over the monolith's whole /app directory, which would have hidden the image's own application code behind an empty volume — fixed to bind-mount only the specific db file and uploads directory. None of the Docker artifacts were build-tested in a real Docker environment (none available here, checked directly) — documented plainly as a first draft to verify, not proven-working, in both the files themselves and a new README Deployment section. New .env.example (a referenced-but-never-created file — only a dangling .gitignore exception for it existed before this).\n\nFull 23-file backend suite + 6-test frontend suite + clean build verified together after every change in this round, not just at the end.",
