    version:  "0.66.0",
    date:     "2026-08-05",
    codename: "Bulkhead",
    summary:  "Four platform-hardening epics (CI Pipeline, Frontend Test Coverage, Runtime Lifecycle Separation, SQLite Ceiling), tracked as real Kanban epics/stories rather than done ad hoc, prompted directly by a 'what else so we don't end up with a crappy monolith' question following v0.65.0/v0.65.1's dead-code work.\n\nEpic: CI Pipeline. Found live that .github/workflows/cypress.yml had zero actual runs ever on GitHub — it only triggered on pull_request, and this project commits directly to main, so it had never once executed. Renamed to ci.yml, added a push-to-main trigger, and added a new backend-tests-and-build job (the full 20-file monolith suite, the 3 document-distribution suites, and a production build) alongside the existing Cypress job. Two more real, previously-uncaught bugs surfaced while building this, both invisible until the workflow could actually run for the first time: scripts/import-mdm-data.js hard-requires cargodesk.db to already exist and the old workflow ran the seed step before starting the server (which creates the DB); and the login rate limiter (20/15min/IP, counts every attempt regardless of success) would blow past its limit partway through a single continuous CI run across 23+ test files each logging in once — the exact friction that forced a manual restart-between-batches workaround throughout this session's own local verification. Fixed the ordering, and made the limiter's threshold configurable via LOGIN_RATE_MAX (default unchanged at 20 for any real deployment; CI sets 200) — verified locally that the full 23-file suite now passes in one continuous run with no restart, and that the unset default still correctly trips at attempt 21.\n\nEpic: Frontend Test Coverage. All existing test assertions were backend integration tests; nothing caught a regression in the frontend at all. Wired up Vitest + Testing Library (already-unused devDependencies) with a mock-the-real-api-module-shape pattern (vi.importActual + auto-wrap every leaf in a resolves-empty-array vi.fn(), rather than hand-listing every method touched, which rots as the app grows) and wrote App.jsx's auth-gating tests (no token -> login page; a rejected token clears itself and falls back; a valid token boots the full shell) plus KanbanPage.jsx's Add Ticket flow end-to-end. Deliberately not covered: KanbanPage's edit/move/delete (behind an unlabeled-icon ActionMenu dropdown, a real separate pre-existing a11y gap) and App.jsx's page-routing logic (lives mostly in child pages via useAuth(), not App() itself) — left as natural next increments. Wired into CI as a third, fully parallel job (no server needed at all).\n\nEpic: Runtime Lifecycle Separation. Audited server.js's four bundled runtime lifecycles (AIS WebSocket listener, OFAC sync scheduler, browser WebSocket broadcast, Puppeteer PDF rendering — full writeup in ARCHITECTURE.md §12) and extracted the one with a genuinely heavy, bursty resource profile that could visibly degrade the other three's latency: PDF rendering, into new services/pdf-render/, CargoDesk's second extracted microservice (port 3003, fully stateless, no database). lib/pdf-signing.js's renderHtmlToPdf(html) kept its exact name/signature — the calling route needed zero changes — only its implementation moved from a local Puppeteer launch to an HTTP call, with the same timeout/clean-error pattern as the document-distribution service's own proxy calls. Cert lookup and cryptographic signing stay in the monolith unchanged: the signing key never leaves the monolith, an existing invariant this extraction preserves exactly. Found and fixed a small inconsistency while wiring the failure path: the generate-document route hardcoded every error to HTTP 500, so an unreachable pdf-render service reported as a generic 500 instead of a proper 503 — fixed to respect the error's own status, verified live. Re-ran the full AIS suite post-extraction (still green, as expected — the AIS listener's own connection handling, including a real past crash-the-process bug and its fix, documented in §12, was never the bottleneck this extraction targeted).\n\nEpic: SQLite Ceiling — design doc + proof-of-concept, per direct scoping (explicitly not a real migration this pass). ARCHITECTURE.md §13 covers why node:sqlite specifically caps future service extraction (no network access, no true concurrent multi-process writers — fine for Document Distribution/PDF Render since neither owns core data, a real blocker for the already-planned Epic 5 Customer/Organization service extraction, which does), what actually changes with Postgres (the driver/query layer is the largest cost by far, not the schema), and a recommendation to sequence it as part of Epic 5 rather than standalone. The proof-of-concept itself (porting system_messages to Postgres) is honestly logged as blocked by this environment, not skipped by choice — no Docker and no native Postgres install available, both checked directly rather than assumed.\n\nAlong the way: ARCHITECTURE.md turned out to be even more stale than src/dev/architecture.html (v0.30.0 vs the app's current version) but its Known Debts table overlapped directly with this session's own work — added a staleness banner, marked three items resolved that are now directly false (no auth -> already resolved earlier; no FK constraints -> foreign_keys=ON has been set globally for a while; no test suite -> now extensive on both backend and frontend), and updated the server.js line-count entry.\n\nFull 23-file backend suite + 6-test frontend suite + clean production build verified together as one continuous pass at the end, all four backend processes (monolith, document-distribution, pdf-render, Vite) running together.",
