    codename: "Ballast",
    summary:  "Follow-up to v0.65.0: executed the first slice of that release's own logged-but-deferred file-breakdown proposal. server.js's ~426-line 'Map functions' section was itself a grab-bag, not purely mappers — genuine pure row-to-API-shape functions (mapShipment, mapContainer, mapCustomer, and 45 others) sat interleaved with real business logic (syncShipmentFromLegs, which writes to the DB; applyShipmentAccessFilter, the role/office/scope authorization filter) that happened to share the same section header. Extracted only the true mappers into new lib/mappers.js, via a createMappers({ portLanesMap, CUTOFF_WARNING_DAYS }) factory — matching the createAisListener({ db, ... }) factory pattern already established in this codebase for a module that needs server.js's own runtime state threaded in rather than importing it directly. mapShipment needs portLanesMap (for tradeLane resolution via longestLane); mapContainer needs CUTOFF_WARNING_DAYS (via cutoffState, for VGM/CY cutoff badges) — both threaded in at factory-creation time rather than each mapper reaching into server.js's module scope. The genuinely non-mapper logic (syncShipmentFromLegs, shipmentMatchesAccessConfig, matchesScopeItem, applyShipmentAccessFilter, and the small policy constants BOOKABLE_CARRIERS/ADDITIONAL_PARTY_ROLES/CUSTOMS_FILING_TYPES) deliberately stayed in server.js rather than being swept into lib/mappers.js for the sake of a bigger line-count win — mislabeling access-control logic as a 'mapper' would have made the codebase harder to navigate, not easier, defeating the actual point of the exercise. server.js: 3230 -> 2984 lines. No behavior changed — every extracted function is byte-identical, only its module location and the mechanism for receiving portLanesMap/CUTOFF_WARNING_DAYS changed (closure -> factory parameter). Full 20-file monolith suite + 3 distribution-service suites green (654 assertions total), clean build, live CDP pass exercising four mapper-heavy pages (Shipments, Customers, Contracts, Kanban) end-to-end with zero console errors.\n\nThe remaining, larger piece of that same file-breakdown proposal (splitting KanbanPage.jsx's ~20 independently-named components into their own files, and the equivalent for App.jsx) was scoped but deliberately not executed this pass, per direct instruction to keep this round to the backend extraction only.",
