    summary:  "Dead-code audit and removal, prompted directly by the architecture-drift review that shipped alongside v0.64.0 (which flagged, but deliberately didn't yet act on, server.js's ~2100 lines of unreachable duplicate routes). The core finding: every one of server.js's own top-level app.get/post/put/patch/delete(...) registrations — 144 of them, everything after the last require('./routes/ais') call — was an exact (method, path) duplicate of a route already registered by routes/*.js. Since Express resolves on first match and the route modules are required first, server.js's own copies were provably unreachable, not just suspected-unused. Confirmed via a purpose-built character-level statement parser (paren-depth tracking that correctly skips over string/template-literal/comment content) rather than a naive line-range heuristic, which the first pass proved necessary — it initially missed 2 of 144 routes on arrow-expression one-liners with no {} block body. Removed all 144, then a further pass found 10 helper functions/constants interleaved in that same dead zone (checkDgPolicy, TICKET_JOIN, saveLegs, saveRates, LEG_TO_MOT, resolveLegPointDead, parseCSVLine, parseOfacCsv, the STALE_EVENTS/mapDoc pair, MAERSK_CODES, mockSailings, maerskSchedules) each independently re-implemented (not shared/imported) inside its respective live routes/*.js file — confirming server.js's own copies were equally dead — plus the now-empty section-header comments left labeling nothing. server.js: 5300 -> 3230 lines (39% smaller), with all critical interleaved infrastructure (the HTTP server, the WebSocket server, PORT/listen) verified still present and correctly positioned throughout.\n\nThe same live-reference-tracing method applied across the rest of the codebase found: src/old - no refactor/ (a full legacy pre-refactor copy of the app, cargodesk.jsx + main.jsx, zero references anywhere) — deleted outright; src/utils/documentGenerator.js (326 lines, client-side jsPDF document generation, zero references — superseded at some earlier point by the server-uploaded DOC_TYPES tracker) — deleted, which let the jspdf/jspdf-autotable dependencies (and 20 transitive packages) drop from package.json entirely; and, found only once the frontend's largest files were being read structurally for the breakdown analysis below, three complete dead component definitions inside DashboardPage.jsx (CarriersPage, ShipmentsPage, ShipmentDetailPage — an entire second, never-rendered, never-exported carrier/shipment list+detail UI, distinct from the real pages under src/pages/shipments/) — 330 more lines removed. Also fixed: CLAUDE.md and AboutPage.jsx both still described 'two independent document systems' via a DocumentsMenu component that had already been fully removed from the codebase at some earlier, undetermined point — both docs corrected to describe the one system that actually exists.\n\nNo behavior changed anywhere in this pass — every removal was proven unreachable or unreferenced before deletion, never inferred. Full existing 20-file monolith suite plus the 3 document-distribution-service/monolith-proxy suites all still green (over 600 assertions, zero failures, run in the established two-batch pattern to stay under the login rate limiter), clean production build throughout every stage of removal, and a live CDP pass confirming login, the Shipments list, a shipment detail page, and the Dashboard all still render correctly with zero console errors against the reduced server.js. A parallel large-file structural survey (server.js plus every routes/*.js and the largest frontend pages, KanbanPage.jsx at 4358 lines / App.jsx at 3977 / ShipmentDetailPage.jsx at 2816 among them) found a consistent, low-risk splitting opportunity — nearly every oversized file is already organized as many independently-named, self-contained components or helpers concatenated into one file, not genuinely tangled logic — logged as a proposed follow-up rather than executed in this pass, since it touches the app's largest and most central files and warrants its own scoping pass.",
