    codename: "Relay",
    summary:  "TKT-SLIRP9 — Document Distribution: EDI + Webhook channels, and CargoDesk's first extracted microservice. The ticket's Email half shipped in v0.52.0 but left EDI and API 'unscoped, for a future pass.' Per direct clarification: EDI means a formal transmittal record (this app's EDI messaging has always been simulated/structured-JSON, with zero attachment concept anywhere in edi_messages, so distributing a document via EDI records metadata + a SHA-256 checksum, not embedded bytes); API means an outbound webhook, paired with a Test-Tools-style simulator mirroring the Message/Filing/AIS Simulator precedent already used three times in this app. Mid-scoping, a standing but not-yet-acted-on goal was restated: CargoDesk's real destination is microservices, not its current Express+node:sqlite monolith — already named as far back as v0.56.0's 'Epic 5: a real Customer/Organization service extraction, sequenced last, only after the data model settles.' Per direct clarification, this ticket became that first real extraction.\n\nNew services/document-distribution/ — a second, genuinely separate deploy unit (own package.json, own port 3002, own SQLite file, same Express+node:sqlite stack as the monolith since there's no reason to introduce a second stack for a first extraction) owning webhook_configs/edi_transmittals/webhook_deliveries. It never touches the monolith's database and never queries shipments/shipment_documents directly — only the opaque ids the monolith hands it, which is the actual ownership boundary. Scope decision: Email stays in the monolith (proven, shipped, zero regression risk to touch for architectural purity alone) — only the two brand-new capabilities get a service boundary from birth. Inter-service auth is a shared static bearer secret (DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_SECRET) — the simplest thing that fits a 2-service, single-operator setup; OAuth2 client-credentials or mTLS would be over-engineering here. The monolith still owns the actual file and the signed document-download link (signToken/verifyToken extracted from routes/share.js into a shared lib/shareToken.js, a pure move) — new GET /api/share/document/:token is a third public carve-out alongside the existing tracking link. No message broker between the two processes — a direct authenticated HTTP call is the right size for two services; an internal channel-handler registry (services/document-distribution/lib/channels.js) gets the same 'add a channel = one new registration' decoupling property without new infrastructure, consistent with the earlier decision (TKT-J0C81B, logged as backlog) to not adopt a heavy engine like Camunda speculatively.\n\nA real bug found live during manual verification: the webhook handler's error path double-inserted the same delivery id (once inside its try block on any non-2xx HTTP response, again in the catch block it then re-threw into), surfacing as a UNIQUE constraint violation instead of the intended clean failure record — fixed by collapsing to exactly one INSERT regardless of outcome. A real UX inconsistency found via the same pass: a never-configured webhook defaulted isActive to false (routes/office-mail.js's own DEFAULT_SETTINGS defaults true for Email) — since the frontend form always sends an explicit value seeded from the GET response, the PUT route's own isActive=true fallback was never actually reachable in practice, so a brand-new webhook silently saved as inactive; fixed by aligning the default to true, matching the sibling feature.\n\nnew SSRF guard (services/document-distribution/lib/webhookSender.js) — https-only, blocks loopback/link-local/private-range literal hosts including the cloud-metadata address — a pragmatic literal-address denylist (not DNS-rebinding-proof), deliberately scoped 'for the time being' per direct clarification. Webhook payloads are HMAC-SHA256 signed (X-CargoDesk-Signature). Test Tools gains a Webhook Simulator tab — a real dev-only mock receiver (not a stub) plus client-side signature verification, so pointing an office's webhook at it proves the whole cross-service, cross-network, signed round trip actually works. Document rows gain '📡 EDI' and '🔗 Webhook' buttons alongside the existing '✉ Send', plus a small bonus fix: a '🕐' history icon wired to the already-generic (but until now never used for documents) EntityHistoryModal, closing a real gap where every send — Email included — wrote an audit event nobody could ever see again.\n\n54 new test assertions across three files (two service-level, hitting port 3002 directly; one monolith-level, exercising the real proxy-to-service round trip — the first test in this suite requiring two processes running, called out explicitly in its own prerequisites). Full existing 20-file/1367-assertion regression suite still green, clean build. Verified live via CDP end-to-end: saved a real office webhook (confirming the isActive fix), sent a document via EDI through the actual UI and confirmed the transmittal in the History modal, and sent via webhook to a real (non-2xx) public host confirming the exact same clean-failure toast the automated tests already proved — no crash, no hang, informative error, on a genuinely separate process reached over a real network call.",
