    codename: "Beacon",
    summary:  "GPS-Coordinate Pickup/Delivery for Classified-Location Customers — some customers' sites (military/government/restricted) can only ever be identified by GPS coordinates, never a normal address or port/UN-LOCODE lookup. Per direct clarification, the fix is a strict either/or per leg endpoint: UN/LOCODE primary OR GPS Coordinates, not a hybrid address+coordinates model (this app never modeled street addresses for pickup/delivery to begin with — a Pick-up/Delivery leg's From/To was always a real UN/LOCODE picked via PortCombobox, with pol_loc_type/pod_loc_type only describing what KIND of location within that port, e.g. Door/Terminal/Container Yard/CFS).\n\nNew customers.classified_location/latitude/longitude (customer Profile tab gains a checkbox that reveals Lat/Lng fields when checked, mirroring the existing credit_hold/credit_hold_reason reveal pattern) — a customer's own canonical GPS-only site. On shipment_legs, pol_loc_type/pod_loc_type gains a fifth value, 'GPS Coordinates', reusing the existing per-endpoint selector rather than a parallel mode column, since it's already the 'what kind of location is this' field — new nullable pol_latitude/pol_longitude/pod_latitude/pod_longitude columns carry the real location when set, and the corresponding pol/pod (UN/LOCODE) is blanked. Explicitly gated to Pick-up/Delivery legs only — a SEA leg always needs a real port, enforced both in the frontend's loc-type option list and server-side in routes/shipments.js's leg POST/PUT (a SEA leg posted with GPS Coordinates is rejected). New validCoord helper (server.js) is the first range validation (-90..90/-180..180) in this codebase for a lat/lng pair — port_locations' own coordinates are trusted/curated import data, never user-typed, so nothing like this existed before.\n\nA real, if narrow, pre-existing gap was fixed along the way, found live via CDP verification of the exact GPS-toggle flow: the SEA-leg gate initially checked a leg's mot field, but ShipmentFormPage.jsx's Leg Type selector only ever updates legType/movementType on change, never mot — so a leg freshly switched from SEA to Pick-up/Delivery in the very same save still carried its old mot='SEA', incorrectly blocking a legitimate GPS-mode switch. Fixed by gating on legType instead, the field the frontend actually keeps authoritative. Also fixed: syncShipmentFromLegs previously set the shipment's own pol/pod unconditionally from its first/last leg — when the bookending Pick-up/Delivery leg is GPS-blanked, this now falls back to the real SEA leg's port (the function already computed this seaLeg fallback for vessel/voyage/carrier, just not pol/pod yet), so generated documents, exports, and list/detail headers keep showing the real ocean port instead of going blank.\n\nDisplay handled via one new shared helper, src/utils/legLocation.js (formatLegPoint), wired into every leg-level rendering surface — LegRow's locked-row port chip and route-summary PKU/DEL cells (ShipmentFormPage.jsx), RouteSummaryBar (ShipmentDetailPage.jsx), and the public TrackingPage.jsx — showing 'GPS: {lat}, {lng}' with a 'Classified location' caption in place of a blank port. One security consideration surfaced directly by this feature and confirmed with the user before shipping: the public, unauthenticated share-token tracking endpoint (GET /api/share/:token) would otherwise leak a classified site's exact coordinates to anyone holding the link — routes/share.js now strips the 4 coordinate fields from that response while keeping the loc-type label, so the public page still shows 'Classified location' rather than a raw blank.\n\nCustomer-driven auto-fill (a classified Shipper/Consignee auto-populating its leg's coordinates) was considered and deliberately deferred — legs and party assignment live on independent lifecycles with no single clean hook point, unlike the existing maybeAssignLineAgents/maybeRescreen precedents; the customer flag and the leg's own GPS mode are independent in this pass, and any leg can be switched to GPS regardless of which customer is attached. New tests/classified-locations.test.js (30 assertions: customer round-trip and out-of-range rejection, classifiedLocation-off force-clearing stored coordinates, leg GPS-mode round-trip and clear-on-switch-back, SEA-leg rejection, the syncShipmentFromLegs SEA-fallback, and the share-token redaction) — full 20-file suite green, clean build. Verified live via CDP end-to-end: flagged a customer as a classified location with real coordinates through the actual Profile tab and confirmed the round-trip; on a real shipment, switched a Pick-up leg to GPS Coordinates, typed in real lat/lng, and confirmed it saved and displayed correctly (including the 'GPS-PT' routing badge and the header's 'GPS / Classified location' card) alongside a real SEA leg whose own port correctly still populated the shipment header.",
