    summary:  "Carrier Line Agents — modeled the CargoWise-baseline relationship between an ocean carrier and its local representative, who differs by port (Maersk's Rotterdam agent isn't its New York agent) and is a distinct concept from a Forwarder's own overseas correspondent network. New carrier_agents master-data table (carrier_code x port_unlocode -> agent customer, no denormalized name column — reads live-join to customers, same idiom CUST_JOIN already uses for parent_customer_name) plus a new Carrier Agents MDM page (src/pages/mdm/MdmCarrierAgentsPage.jsx, modeled directly on MdmLinkedPortsPage.jsx's list+add-modal shape). port_unlocode gets a real FK (matching linked_ports' own style); carrier_code deliberately stays loose text, matching the established convention for that column everywhere else in the schema (shipments/contracts/allocations never FK it either); agent_customer_id is a real FK with no ON DELETE clause — neither CASCADE (would let a customer delete silently destroy master data) nor SET NULL (would leave a meaningless NOT NULL-in-spirit row) fit, so customer delete is instead blocked by a new app-level guard in routes/customers.js, mirroring offices.js's own 'referenced by shipments — deactivate it instead' pattern.\n\nRather than building a parallel party-management system, the resolved agent writes into the *existing*, already-generic shipment_parties mechanism as two new roles — 'Line Agent (Export)' and 'Line Agent (Import)' — directly mirroring how 'Customs Broker' was already split Export/Import in this exact codebase for the identical reason (one shipment, two ends, one role slot each per the UNIQUE(shipment_id, role) constraint). New resolveCarrierAgent(carrierCode, portUnlocode) helper (server.js, beside linkedPortCodes) tries a direct match first, then falls back through linkedPortCodes — the same linked-port-aware matching findMatchingContractLeg already uses — so an agent registered against a seaport still resolves for a shipment routed via a linked inland ICD. maybeAssignLineAgents (routes/shipments.js) fires on shipment create (before importContractRates/screenShipmentById, so a sanctioned agent is never missed on day one) and on carrier/POL/POD change on update — the UNIQUE constraint's own violation-on-insert IS the 'only fill an empty slot, never overwrite' mechanism (same idiom the existing additional-party POST route already relies on), so a manually-assigned or previously-auto-assigned party is never silently clobbered, and a carrier change to one with no registered agent simply leaves the existing party untouched rather than auto-removing it.\n\nBecause it's an ordinary shipment_parties row once created, everything else picks it up for free with zero additional code: compliance screening (screenShipmentById already iterates all party roles generically), and the customer role-derivation/segmented Trading-Customers/Service-Providers list (v0.60.0) — both new roles land under Service Providers automatically the first time any customer is actually used that way. The one deliberate new UI surface is a read-only Line Agents card on the shipment's Carrier Booking -> Details tab (src/pages/shipments/ShipmentCarrierBookingDetailsPage.jsx), placed right after the existing Carrier/Route/Vessel card since that's where the booking desk already works and where carrier+POL+POD are already surfaced — sourced from the same shipmentParties.list() call AdditionalPartiesPanel already uses, with a 'Not registered' fallback per side and a caption pointing to Parties & Offices for one-off reassignment.\n\nTwo interactive HTML mockups were built and reviewed before writing code, per explicit request — the first covering the new Carrier Agents MDM page, the second revised mid-review from an Additional-Parties-panel concept to the actual Carrier Booking Details placement once that was identified as the right surface. New tests/carrier-agents.test.js (24 assertions: CRUD, the customer-delete guard, and the full shipment-integration path — auto-assignment on create, no duplication on unrelated edits, no auto-removal when a carrier change has no registered agent, and a manually-assigned party never overwritten by a later auto-resolution) — full 19-file suite green, clean build. Verified thoroughly via the automated suite and direct API checks confirming the exact data the two new UI surfaces depend on renders correctly; live browser-screenshot verification of the new MDM page and Carrier Booking card specifically was inconclusive due to intermittent dev-proxy connectivity issues in this environment unrelated to the change itself (confirmed by the backend responding correctly to every direct request throughout).",
