    date:     "2026-08-01",
    codename: "Transponder",
    summary:  "Direct design correction to the just-shipped AIS feature, plus a dense round of live bug fixes found while re-verifying the Route Legs table and Vessels MDM against it. The v0.55.0 design showed AIS-detected departure/arrival as a separate, always-visible ATD/ATA pair alongside ETD/ETA — direct correction: 'we are displaying the ATD and ATA instead of dinamycally changing the ETD and ETA when we receive a schedule event for confirmed departure or arrival.' Reworked: a confirmed departure/arrival now updates etd/eta on the SEA leg IN PLACE (an estimate becoming a known fact), not a separate column. New shipment_legs.etd_source/eta_source ('manual'|'ais'|'') replace the old atd/ata/atd_source/ata_source columns as the write model — the old columns are left in the DB, inert, per this codebase's standing no-migration-cleanup precedent, not dropped. The write guard changed shape to match: not a blank-fill (etd/eta are almost always already populated with an estimate, so a blank-check would never fire) but an idempotent-confirmation check (`etd_source==='ais'` means already-confirmed, don't re-fire) — and unlike the old atd/ata design, this means the FIRST AIS confirmation legitimately overwrites the prior estimate, and a subsequent MANUAL correction legitimately overwrites an AIS-confirmed value right back, clearing the source flag. A small ship-icon indicator on the ETD/ETA cells (Route Legs table) marks a confirmed value; editing the date clears it. lib/ais-listener.js, the leg CRUD routes (routes/shipments.js), routes/ais.js's open-legs query, and the Test Tools AIS Simulator's Position tab were all reworked to match; tests/ais-integration.test.js rewritten around the corrected model (etd/eta assertions, AIS_DEPARTURE_CONFIRMED/AIS_ARRIVAL_CONFIRMED event names, a new 'manual correction overwrites AIS-confirmed value' test replacing the old 'manual atd is never overwritten' one) — 30 assertions, full suite green.\n\nFour real, unrelated bugs surfaced and fixed during the same feedback pass, all direct user reports against real shipments: (1) the Carrier column in the Route Legs table could visually overlap Movement By next to it ('the movement by is overalapped by the carrier column') — root cause was a classic CSS flexbox min-width:auto interaction: CarrierCombobox's own root div had no explicit min-width, so as a flex child it refused to shrink below its selected chip's intrinsic content width (e.g. 'MAEU Maersk Line' fully spelled) even inside a fixed-width table cell with overflow:hidden set on the wrapper. Fixed at the shared-component source (CarrierCombobox.jsx gets minWidth:0 on its own root div, benefiting every usage app-wide, not just this one table) plus overflow:hidden on the cell wrapper. (2) Header/row column misalignment, reported repeatedly across several rounds before being root-caused: the locked/read-only LegRow branch (used once a real schedule is assigned) renders a leading lock-icon column of variable width (0/18/40px depending on state) that the header had no matching placeholder for, while the editable branch rendered no leading column at all — so column x-positions never lined up consistently between the header and either row type. Fixed with one shared LEG_LEAD_COL_W=40 constant reserved identically in the header, the locked branch (icon shown or not), and the editable branch (always blank) — verified via a fresh CDP screenshot showing the header's vertical separator bars now landing exactly on each column's boundary in the row data below. (3) The Sailing Search modal appeared to show a duplicate schedule entry with degraded data (0d transit, no legs) alongside a correct one — not actually duplicate data: the shipment's own previously-committed schedule copy predated an earlier transit_days fix and had been permanently frozen at 0 (fixed by broadening the startup backfill migration to also cover source='search' rows, not just source='generated' template rows), and separately neither catalogSailings()'s search results nor POST .../schedules' commit route ever carried vesselImo through at all (both fixed) — three compounding gaps that together looked like one duplicate-entry bug. (4) A module-level LEG_TYPE_COLOR object in ShipmentFormPage.jsx captured T.accent/T.info/T.textMuted once at import time — since T's colors are mutated in place on theme toggle rather than the object being rebuilt, and that import happens before App's own theme-restore effect ever runs, the 'Delivery' leg type label was permanently stuck rendering in the dark theme's blue-gray textMuted on every load, light mode included ('fix the gray text color in light mode and set it to black'). Fixed by converting it to a function evaluated at render time, so it always reads T's current values.\n\nAlso in the Vessels MDM page (from the same session's earlier AIS vessel-import work): row heights now stay consistent regardless of whether a vessel has flag data (the Flag cell always reserves two stacked lines); the Actions column header is now right-aligned to match its right-aligned button content; and the Flag badge's caption text now gets the same 9px left inset Badge.jsx's own internal padding gives the badge above it, fixing a subtle optical (not layout) misalignment between the two. Full npm test suite green (14 files), clean vite build. Also directly verified live: the remove-leg-cascades-to-remove-the-whole-schedule behavior (separately reported as broken) was reproduced on a disposable clone of the real reference shipment's exact leg/schedule shape rather than on the real shipment itself, and worked correctly end-to-end (2 legs -> 0, 1 schedule -> 0, correct unlink toast) — the cascade logic itself was not broken.",
