    summary:  "Decoupled the Schedule Generator (Test Tools) from shipments, direct request. Previously a generated schedule could only be created by simultaneously forcing a link to at least one real shipment (shipment_schedules.shipment_id was NOT NULL) — and the everyday Add Sailing search never looked at that stored catalog at all, always synthesizing either live Maersk results or fully fake 'DEMO ...' sailings. Generate now just stores a schedule — no shipment picker, create-time or post-hoc — and Add Sailing search checks the catalog first (matching POL, POD, transshipment hub(s), ETD/ETA window, and loop/service code), falling back to live/demo data only when nothing real matches. A one-time guarded table-rebuild migration (SQLite can't drop NOT NULL via ALTER TABLE) made shipment_id nullable and added a self-referential template_id column — a shipment picking a catalog match copies it into its own row, recording which template it came from, rather than a shared-ownership/link model; the old schedule_shipment_links-based link/unlink routes are gone. Real TSP/multi-leg support was added in the same pass — a new 'Configure Legs' modal in the Generator (a leg-rows table styled after the existing Route Legs add/remove-row interaction, direct request over a bespoke inline multi-field form) builds a genuine multi-leg sailing backed by a new schedule_legs child table (mirrors the existing shipment_legs/contract_legs multi-leg pattern); the parent row's own pol/vessel/etd (first leg) and pod/eta (last leg) keep summarizing the overall journey so every existing schedule consumer (the shipment header's Loop field, etc.) is unaffected. New demo_schedules_enabled setting (App Settings -> API Controls -> External APIs, default on) gates the synthetic mock fallback specifically — live carrier API results are unaffected, since they're real, not demo; defaulting on means search/testing keeps working with zero setup until real generated schedules exist to prefer instead. A gap surfaced while writing tests: with no owning shipment, the existing per-shipment schedule-delete route could never reach a template at all — added a dedicated DELETE /api/schedules/:id route (and a delete button in the now-read-only catalog browser) to close it. tests/schedule-catalog.test.js rewritten around the new contract (50 assertions: ownerless creation, TSP leg derivation, usage/template provenance, the old link routes returning 404, catalog-first search priority, and the toggle's on/off behavior) — full suite green (13 files, 516 assertions), clean build. Verified live end-to-end via a temporary Cypress spec: built a real 2-leg TSP schedule through the Configure Legs modal, confirmed it in the read-only catalog browser, found it via Add Sailing search on a real shipment tagged 'Catalog', picked it and confirmed both SEA legs were created correctly with the right POL/hub/POD chain and templateId provenance, and confirmed the Settings toggle renders checked by default.",
